    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite is green. Here's a summary of what I changed:\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change adds a structured address form (`addressLine1`, optional `addressLine2`, plus the existing `city`/`postcode`) to the owner-create flow, while keeping the flat `address` input working for backward compatibility.\n\n**API contract** (`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`)\n- Added `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` string properties to the `OwnerFields` schema (which the `Owner` response schema extends, so both appear on request and response).\n- Removed `address` from the `required` list so a structured owner that omits the flat `address` isn't rejected by bean validation before reaching the controller. Documented that `address` is always returned as the composed, normalized value.\n\n**Domain model** (`model/Owner.java`)\n- Added `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` columns and their getters/setters. MapStruct maps them automatically both ways.\n\n**Controller** (`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`)\n- Rewrote `applyAddress` to prefer the structured fields: when a non-blank `addressLine1` is supplied it normalizes both lines (same `normalizeAddress` rules \u2014 upper-case, whitespace collapse, `ST\u2192STREET`, etc.) and composes the flat `address` as normalized `addressLine1`, appending a single space and normalized `addressLine2` only when `addressLine2` is present. Otherwise it normalizes the flat `address` as before.\n- Because the effective address is composed into the `address` field before validation and persistence, everything downstream (the required-field check, the stored/returned address, household hash, postcode validation, locality) transparently uses the structured value when present and falls back to the flat address.\n\n**DB schemas** \u2014 added `address_line1`/`address_line2` columns to the owners table in all four dialects (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests, h2, mysql, postgres).\n\n**Result:** `./mvnw -o test` \u2192 `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` / `BUILD SUCCESS`. The structured test (`10 king st` \u2192 `addressLine1` and composed `address` both `10 KING STREET`) and the flat-address backward-compat test both pass.",
